.TH ANONYHOST 1 "2026" "AnonyHost" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anonyhost, anonyhost-stop \- host a website as a Tor onion service from your own computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anonyhost
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B anonyhost-stop
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B anonyhost
starts the AnonyHost dashboard, a web interface that only listens on 127.0.0.1, and opens it in a window
or your default browser. Only one dashboard runs per user.
.PP
.B anonyhost-stop
stops the dashboard and the hosted website (Tor and nginx).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Stay attached to the terminal instead of detaching.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-browser
Do not open a window or browser.
.TP
.B \-\-stop
Stop the dashboard. Hosting keeps running.
.TP
.B \-\-stop\-all
Stop the dashboard and the hosted website.
.TP
.B \-\-reset\-password
Remove the administrator account. A new one is created at the next launch.
.TP
.B \-\-factory\-reset
Return AnonyHost to its first-run state (asks for confirmation). The onion keys are kept.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-keys
With \-\-factory\-reset, also delete the onion keys. The .onion address is lost for good.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/anonyhost
Your website, settings, logs and onion keys (or the directory in $ANONYHOST_DATA). Never removed by the package.
.TP
.I /opt/anonyhost
The program.
.TP
.I /etc/anonyhost/keep-system-services
If this file exists when the package is installed, the system tor and nginx services are left untouched.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR nginx (8)
